\fBOrigin\fR
Onesimus extension. The Bareos Director does not know this command and answers "is an invalid command".
.SS "\&.grammar"
Print the console grammar (Onesimus)
.PP
\fBSynopsis\fR
.nf
\&.grammar [command=<command>]
.fi
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Prints the grammar of every command the console may run (CommandACL): subcommand words, keywords, key=value arguments, value sources, repeatable keys and exclusive groups. In API mode 2 the reply is the JSON object "grammar" (schema 1), otherwise one usage line per command and subcommand.
.PP
oconsole loads it at connect for completion and hints. The Bareos Director does not know the command and answers "is an invalid command"; clients then fall back to .help.
.PP
\fBArguments\fR
.PP
\fI\&.grammar\fR
.TP
.B "command=<command>"
Only this command (name; a command name)
.PP
\fBExamples\fR
.TP
.B "\&.api 2"
switch to JSON
.TP
.B "\&.grammar"
the grammar of every permitted command
.TP
.B "\&.grammar command=purge"
only purge
.TP
.B "\&.grammar command=list"
only the grammar of list and its subcommands
.PP
\fBOrigin\fR
Onesimus extension. The Bareos Director does not know this command and answers "is an invalid command".
.SS "\&.bvfs_update"
Update BVFS cache
.PP
\fBSynopsis\fR
.nf
\&.bvfs_update [jobid=<jobid>[,...]] [catalog=<catalog>]
.fi
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Fills the BVFS cache in the catalog (the tables PathHierarchy and PathVisibility and the flag Job.HasCache). Without this cache .bvfs_lsdirs and .bvfs_lsfiles find no directories. With jobid= only the listed jobs are prepared; without it all jobs without cache are, which can take long on large catalogs. Jobs that already have a cache are skipped. Backup data is not changed.
.PP
On success nothing is printed. BVFS is kept unchanged for compatibility with Bareos tools such as bareos\-webui; the OVFS commands need no cache.
.PP
\fBArguments\fR
.PP
\fI\&.bvfs_update\fR
.TP
.B "jobid=<jobid>[,...]"
Job IDs (text; several values separated by commas)
.TP
.B "catalog=<catalog>"
Catalog name (name; values from .catalogs)
.PP
\fBExamples\fR
.TP
.B "\&.bvfs_update jobid=600,615,616"
build the cache for one job chain
.TP
.B "\&.bvfs_update"
build the cache for all jobs without one
.PP
\fBOrigin\fR
Bareos console command.
.SS "\&.bvfs_cleanup"
Cleanup BVFS cache for a certain handle
.PP
\fBSynopsis\fR
.nf
\&.bvfs_cleanup path=<path>
.fi
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Drops the table that .bvfs_restore created for a restore selection, after the restore was started with file=?<table> or abandoned. path= names the table (b2 followed by a number); other names are skipped silently and a missing table is not an error. The BVFS cache stays.
.PP
\fBArguments\fR
.PP
\fI\&.bvfs_cleanup\fR
.TP
.B "path=<path>"
Handle (table name) (text; required)
.PP
\fBExamples\fR
.TP
.B "\&.bvfs_cleanup path=b2101"
drop a restore selection table
.PP
\fBOrigin\fR
Bareos console command.
.SS "\&.bvfs_clear_cache"
Clear BVFS cache
.PP
\fBSynopsis\fR
.nf
\&.bvfs_clear_cache [yes]
.fi
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Clears the whole BVFS cache in the catalog: Job.HasCache is reset for all jobs and the tables PathHierarchy and PathVisibility are emptied. The keyword yes is required; without it nothing happens and there is no confirmation prompt. Afterwards the cache must be rebuilt with .bvfs_update. Backup data and file records are not touched.
.PP
\fBArguments\fR
.PP
\fI\&.bvfs_clear_cache\fR
.TP
.B "yes"
Confirm (keyword)
.PP
\fBExamples\fR
.TP
.B "\&.bvfs_clear_cache yes"
empty the BVFS cache
.PP
\fBOrigin\fR
Bareos console command.
.SS "\&.bvfs_get_jobids"
Get jobids required for a restore
.PP
\fBSynopsis\fR
.nf
\&.bvfs_get_jobids [jobid=<jobid>] [ujobid=<ujobid>] [all]
.fi
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Returns the chain of JobIds needed to restore the state of a job: the Full backup, the last Differential and all Incrementals up to that job, as one comma separated line. The list is the jobid= argument of the other .bvfs commands. ujobid= gives the job by its unique job name instead and takes precedence. all combines the chains of all filesets of the client.
.PP
For a Base job only its own JobId is returned. JobIds the console may not see by its job, client or fileset ACL are left out. The command only reads the catalog; the OVFS counterpart is .ovfs_latest.
.PP
\fBArguments\fR
.PP
\fI\&.bvfs_get_jobids\fR
.TP
.B "jobid=<jobid>"
Job ID (number)
.TP
.B "ujobid=<ujobid>"
Unique job name (text)
.TP
.B "all"
Every job of the chain (keyword)
.PP
\fBExamples\fR
.TP
.B "\&.bvfs_get_jobids jobid=628"
the job chain for restoring job 628
.TP
.B "\&.bvfs_get_jobids jobid=628 all"
the chains of all filesets of the client
.PP
\fBOrigin\fR
Bareos console command.
.SS "\&.bvfs_lsdirs"
List directories using BVFS
.PP
\fBSynopsis\fR
.nf
\&.bvfs_lsdirs [jobid=<jobid>[,...]] [path=<path>] [pathid=<pathid>] [limit=<limit>] [offset=<offset>] [pattern=<pattern>] [filename=<filename>]
.fi
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Lists the subdirectories of a directory in the state of a job chain, as a restore file browser needs them. The BVFS cache must have been filled with .bvfs_update first. The directory is given by path= (with a trailing slash, / for the root) or by pathid= from an earlier output; limit= (default 2000) and offset= page through the result.
.PP
Each line has five tab separated columns: PathId, FileId, JobId, LStat and name. The first two lines are . and ..; directories without their own catalog record have FileId and JobId 0. A JobId the console may not see is refused.
.PP
\fBArguments\fR
.PP
\fI\&.bvfs_lsdirs\fR
.TP
.B "jobid=<jobid>[,...]"
Job IDs (text; several values separated by commas)
.TP
.B "path=<path>"
Directory (text)
.TP
.B "pathid=<pathid>"
Path ID (number)
.TP
.B "limit=<limit>"
Most rows (number)
.TP
.B "offset=<offset>"
Rows to skip (number)
.TP
.B "pattern=<pattern>"
Name filter (regular expression) (text)
.TP
.B "filename=<filename>"
File name (text)
.PP
\fBExamples\fR
.TP
.B "\&.bvfs_lsdirs jobid=600,615,616 path=/"
the top level directories of a job chain
.TP
.B "\&.bvfs_lsdirs jobid=600,615,616 pathid=7 limit=100"
the first 100 subdirectories of a known path
.PP
\fBOrigin\fR
Bareos console command.
.SS "\&.bvfs_lsfiles"
List files using BVFS
.PP
\fBSynopsis\fR
.nf
\&.bvfs_lsfiles [jobid=<jobid>[,...]] [path=<path>] [pathid=<pathid>] [limit=<limit>] [offset=<offset>] [pattern=<pattern>] [filename=<filename>]
.fi
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Lists the files (without subdirectories) of a directory in the state of a job chain; for each file the version from the newest job of the chain is shown. The FileIds are the input for .bvfs_restore. The BVFS cache must have been filled with .bvfs_update first. pattern= keeps only file names matching a regular expression; limit= (default 2000) and offset= page through the result.
.PP
Each line has five tab separated columns: PathId, FileId, JobId, LStat and file name.
.PP
\fBArguments\fR
.PP
\fI\&.bvfs_lsfiles\fR
.TP
.B "jobid=<jobid>[,...]"
Job IDs (text; several values separated by commas)
.TP
.B "path=<path>"
Directory (text)
.TP
.B "pathid=<pathid>"
Path ID (number)
.TP
.B "limit=<limit>"
Most rows (number)
.TP
.B "offset=<offset>"
Rows to skip (number)
.TP
.B "pattern=<pattern>"
Name filter (regular expression) (text)
.TP
.B "filename=<filename>"
File name (text)
.PP
\fBExamples\fR
.TP
.B "\&.bvfs_lsfiles jobid=600,615,616 path=/etc/"
the files of /etc in a job chain
.TP
.B "\&.bvfs_lsfiles jobid=600,615,616 pathid=7 pattern=conf"
only file names matching a pattern
.PP
\fBOrigin\fR
Bareos console command.
.SS "\&.bvfs_versions"
List versions of a file
.PP
\fBSynopsis\fR
.nf
\&.bvfs_versions [jobid=<jobid>] [client=<client>] [pathid=<pathid>] [path=<path>] [filename=<filename>] [fname=<fname>] [name=<name>] [copies] [versions] [limit=<limit>] [offset=<offset>]
.fi
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Lists all backed up versions of one file of a client across all jobs, each with its volume, so an older version can be picked for .bvfs_restore by its FileId. Only versions stored on a volume count. jobid= is required but not evaluated (by Bareos convention 0); the directory is given by path= or pathid=, the file by fname= (also filename= or name=). copies includes versions from copy jobs; versions is accepted but has no effect.
.PP
Each line has seven tab separated columns: PathId, FileId, JobId, LStat, MD5, volume name and InChanger.
.PP
\fBArguments\fR
.PP
\fI\&.bvfs_versions\fR
.TP
.B "jobid=<jobid>"
Job ID (text)
.TP
.B "client=<client>"
Client name (name; values from .clients)
.TP
.B "pathid=<pathid>"
Path ID (number)
.TP
.B "path=<path>"
Directory (text)
.TP
.B "filename=<filename>"
File name (text)
.TP
.B "fname=<fname>"
File name (text)
.TP
.B "name=<name>"
File name (text)
.TP
.B "copies"
Include copies (keyword)
.TP
.B "versions"
Every version (keyword)
.TP
.B "limit=<limit>"
Most rows (number)
.TP
.B "offset=<offset>"
Rows to skip (number)
.PP
\fBExamples\fR
.TP
.B "\&.bvfs_versions jobid=0 client=bareos\-fd path=/etc/ fname=hosts"
all versions of /etc/hosts
.TP
.B "\&.bvfs_versions jobid=0 client=bareos\-fd path=/etc/ fname=hosts copies"
including versions from copy jobs
.PP
\fBOrigin\fR
Bareos console command.
.SS "\&.bvfs_restore"
Mark BVFS files/directories for restore. Stored in handle.
.PP
\fBSynopsis\fR
.nf
\&.bvfs_restore path=<path> jobid=<jobid>[,...] [fileid=<fileid>[,...]] [dirid=<dirid>[,...]] [hardlink=<hardlink>[,...]] [useemptypath]
.fi
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Creates a table in the catalog holding a restore selection: single files (fileid=), whole directories with their content (dirid=, PathIds from .bvfs_lsdirs) and hardlinks (hardlink=, pairs of JobId and FileIndex). At least one of them is required. path= names the table (b2 followed by a number); an existing table of that name is replaced.
.PP
Pass the table to restore with file=?<table>; restore then skips the menu and the file tree. Drop the table afterwards with .bvfs_cleanup. The command only writes to the catalog and starts no job.
.PP
\fBArguments\fR
.PP
\fI\&.bvfs_restore\fR
.TP
.B "path=<path>"
Handle (table name) (text; required)
.TP
.B "jobid=<jobid>[,...]"
Job IDs (text; several values separated by commas; required)
.TP
.B "fileid=<fileid>[,...]"
File IDs (text; several values separated by commas)
.TP
.B "dirid=<dirid>[,...]"
Directory IDs (text; several values separated by commas)
.TP
.B "hardlink=<hardlink>[,...]"
JobId,FileIndex pairs (text; several values separated by commas)
.TP
.B "useemptypath"
Use an empty path (keyword)
.PP
\fBExamples\fR
.TP
.B "\&.bvfs_restore path=b2101 jobid=600,615,616 fileid=4711,4712"
select two files for a restore
.TP
.B "\&.bvfs_restore path=b2102 jobid=600,615,616 dirid=7"
select a whole directory
.PP
\fBOrigin\fR
Bareos console command.
.SS "\&.config"
Read, create, change, delete configuration resources as raw text (Onesimus)
.PP
\fBSynopsis\fR
.nf
\&.config [json]
\&.config capabilities
\&.config list type=<type>
\&.config read type=<type> name=<name>
\&.config create type=<type> text64=<text64> [validate_only]
\&.config update type=<type> name=<name> sha256=<sha256> text64=<text64> [validate_only]
\&.config delete type=<type> name=<name> sha256=<sha256> [validate_only]
.fi
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Reads, creates, changes and deletes configuration resources as raw text (the same logic as the DirectorApi configuration RPCs). Changes need the CommandACL configure and the SHA\-256 of the text read.
.PP
\fBSubcommands\fR
.TP
.B "\&.config capabilities"
Editable types and operations
.IP
Shows the interface version and, per resource type, the operations the configuration CRUD offers (READ_RAW, CREATE_RAW, UPDATE_RAW, DELETE). The editable types are schedule, job, jobdefs and client. Without the CommandACL configure no types are listed.
.TP
.B "\&.config list"
Resources of a type
.IP
Lists the resources of one type (schedule, job, jobdefs or client) with their file, SHA\-256 and whether the file holds more than one resource. Resources the console may not see by its resource ACL are left out.
.TP
.B "\&.config read"
Raw text of a resource
.IP
Returns the raw text of one resource byte for byte, with its file and SHA\-256. The checksum is the optimistic lock for a later update or delete. A resource the console may not see gives the status DENIED.
.TP
.B "\&.config create"
New resource from text
.IP
Creates a resource from its raw text, given base64 encoded in text64= because a console line carries no line ends. The new configuration is checked like odir \-t, written atomically and reloaded, with rollback if the reload fails. With validate_only the text is only checked, nothing is written.
.IP
A result status other than OK (EXISTS, INVALID, DENIED ...) is reported in the response, not as a command error. Needs the CommandACL configure.
.TP
.B "\&.config update"
Replace a resource
.IP
Replaces the raw text of a resource. sha256= must be the checksum from the last read; if the file changed since, the status is CONFLICT and nothing is written. The text is checked like odir \-t, written atomically and reloaded with rollback; validate_only only checks. Needs the CommandACL configure.
.TP
.B "\&.config delete"
Delete a resource
.IP
Deletes a resource, guarded by the checksum from the last read. If other resources still refer to it the status is REFERENCED, the referring resources are listed and nothing is deleted. validate_only only checks. Needs the CommandACL configure.
.PP
\fBArguments\fR
.PP
\fI\&.config\fR
.TP
.B "json"
Print the JSON object as text (keyword)
.PP
\fI\&.config list\fR
.TP
.B "type=<type>"
Resource type (one of a fixed set; values: client, job, jobdefs, schedule, fileset, pool, storage; required)
.PP
\fI\&.config read\fR
.TP
.B "type=<type>"
Resource type (one of a fixed set; values: client, job, jobdefs, schedule, fileset, pool, storage; required)
.TP
.B "name=<name>"
Resource name (text; required)
.PP
\fI\&.config create\fR
.TP
.B "type=<type>"
Resource type (one of a fixed set; values: client, job, jobdefs, schedule, fileset, pool, storage; required)
.TP
.B "text64=<text64>"
Resource text, base64 (text; required)
.TP
.B "validate_only"
Check only, write nothing (keyword)
.PP
\fI\&.config update\fR
.TP
.B "type=<type>"
Resource type (one of a fixed set; values: client, job, jobdefs, schedule, fileset, pool, storage; required)
.TP
.B "name=<name>"
Resource name (text; required)
.TP
.B "sha256=<sha256>"
SHA\-256 of the text read (text; required)
.TP
.B "text64=<text64>"
Resource text, base64 (text; required)
.TP
.B "validate_only"
Check only, write nothing (keyword)
.PP
\fI\&.config delete\fR
.TP
.B "type=<type>"
Resource type (one of a fixed set; values: client, job, jobdefs, schedule, fileset, pool, storage; required)
.TP
.B "name=<name>"
Resource name (text; required)
.TP
.B "sha256=<sha256>"
SHA\-256 of the text read (text; required)
.TP
.B "validate_only"
Check only, write nothing (keyword)
.PP
\fBExamples\fR
.TP
.B "\&.config list type=job json"
the job resources as one JSON line
.TP
.B "\&.config read type=schedule name=WeeklyCycle"
the raw text of a schedule with its checksum
.TP
.B "\&.config capabilities"
the editable types and their operations
.TP
.B "\&.config list type=client"
all client resources with file and checksum
.TP
.B "\&.config read type=client name=bareos\-fd"
the raw client text and its checksum
.TP
.B "\&.config create type=schedule text64=U2NoZWR1bGUgewogIE5hbWUgPSBOaWdodGx5CiAgUnVuID0gRnVsbCBzdW4gYXQgMjM6MDAKfQo validate_only"
check a new schedule without writing it
.TP
.B "\&.config update type=schedule name=Nightly sha256=5b1e9a3c text64=U2NoZWR1bGUgewogIE5hbWUgPSBOaWdodGx5CiAgUnVuID0gRnVsbCBzYXQgYXQgMjI6MDAKfQo"
replace a schedule with new text
.TP
.B "\&.config delete type=client name=old\-fd sha256=5b1e9a3c validate_only"
check whether a client could be deleted
.PP
\fBOrigin\fR
Onesimus extension. The Bareos Director does not know this command and answers "is an invalid command".
.SS "\&.volume_damaged"
Damaged Volume: affected jobs, copies, new Full backups (Onesimus)
.PP
\fBSynopsis\fR
.nf
\&.volume_damaged volume=<volume> [dryrun] [yes] [full]
.fi
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Onesimus extension: the flow for a damaged Volume (tape tear, disk failure, a broken file Volume). Without yes (or with dryrun) it only reports: the jobs that have data on the Volume (a job over several Volumes is lost as a whole, the other Volumes are named), for each job whether a usable copy exists (a finished Backup or Copy job of the same original that lies on no damaged Volume and no Volume with status Error), the Full backups to start for the jobs without a copy, and the later Incremental and Differential jobs of a damaged Full.
.PP
With yes the Volume is set to VolStatus Error and the damaged jobs that have a usable copy are purged; the oldest copy becomes a Backup job (UpgradeCopies), so a restore finds it. Jobs without a copy stay in the catalog. full additionally starts a Full of every job without a copy.
.PP
\fBArguments\fR
.PP
\fI\&.volume_damaged\fR
.TP
.B "volume=<volume>"
Volume name (text; required)
.TP
.B "dryrun"
Only report (keyword)
.TP
.B "yes"
Set the Volume to Error and purge the damaged jobs that have a copy (keyword)
.TP
.B "full"
With yes: start a Full of the jobs without a copy (keyword)
.PP
\fBExamples\fR
.TP
.B "\&.volume_damaged volume=Vol\-0007"
what a damaged Volume costs
.TP
.B "\&.volume_damaged volume=Vol\-0007 yes full"
set it to Error, promote the copies, start new Fulls
.PP
\fBOrigin\fR
Onesimus extension. The Bareos Director does not know this command and answers "is an invalid command".
.SS "\&.ovfs_status"
Show whether restore trees use the ovfs extension or the Director fallback
.PP
\fBSynopsis\fR
.nf
\&.ovfs_status [refresh] [json]
.fi
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Onesimus extension: shows whether the PostgreSQL extension ovfs is installed and usable in the catalog database and which path answers the .ovfs commands, the extension or the equivalent plain SQL fallback in the Director. Both give the same results; the extension is faster on large catalogs. If the extension is not usable the reason is shown.
.PP
refresh checks the extension again instead of showing the stored result. json prints the result as one JSON line in every API mode.
.PP
\fBArguments\fR
.PP
\fI\&.ovfs_status\fR
.TP
.B "refresh"
Check the extension again now (keyword)
.TP
.B "json"
Print the JSON object as text (keyword)
.PP
\fBExamples\fR
.TP
.B "\&.ovfs_status"
extension state and restore tree path
.TP
.B "\&.ovfs_status refresh json"
check again and print JSON
.PP
\fBOrigin\fR
Onesimus extension. The Bareos Director does not know this command and answers "is an invalid command".
.SS "\&.ovfs_latest"
Job chain of a client's latest state (restore option 5)
.PP
\fBSynopsis\fR
.nf
\&.ovfs_latest client=<client> [fileset=<fileset>] [json]
.fi
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Onesimus extension: determines the job chain for the newest state of a client, as restore menu item 5 selects it: the last Full backup of the fileset, the last Differential after it and all Incrementals after Full or Differential. Only backups with status T or W count, Differentials and Incrementals only if they are on an enabled volume. fileset= is needed when the client has backups of several filesets.
.PP
Pass the comma separated list as jobid= to .ovfs_tree and .ovfs_ls, so the tree stays the same while browsing even if a new backup finishes. json prints the result as one JSON line.
.PP
\fBArguments\fR
.PP
\fI\&.ovfs_latest\fR
.TP
.B "client=<client>"
Client name (name; values from .clients; required)
.TP
.B "fileset=<fileset>"
FileSet name (name; values from .filesets)
.TP
.B "json"
Print the JSON object as text (keyword)
.PP
\fBExamples\fR
.TP
.B "\&.ovfs_latest client=bareos\-fd"
the newest job chain of a client
.TP
.B "\&.ovfs_latest client=bareos\-fd fileset=LinuxAll json"
the chain of one fileset as JSON
.PP
\fBOrigin\fR
Onesimus extension. The Bareos Director does not know this command and answers "is an invalid command".
.SS "\&.ovfs_tree"
Restore tree of a job chain as JSON (OVFS)
.PP
\fBSynopsis\fR
.nf
\&.ovfs_tree [jobid=<jobid>[,...]] [client=<client>] [fileset=<fileset>] [root=<root>] [depth=<depth>] [max_entries=<max_entries>] [json]
.fi
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Onesimus extension: returns the directory tree of a job chain below a directory as a restore would bring it back: per path the version from the newest job, deleted files left out, hardlinks and delta files resolved. Every node with a catalog record carries its restore identity (jobid, fileindex). No cache is needed. The chain is given by jobid= or by client= (and fileset=) for the newest state like .ovfs_latest.
.PP
root= sets the top of the tree (default /), depth= the number of levels (default 1; directories at the limit show their entry count), max_entries= the maximum number of nodes (default 100000; beyond it the command stops with a message). For large backups load the top with a small depth and expand directories with .ovfs_ls. json prints one JSON line.
.PP
\fBArguments\fR
.PP
\fI\&.ovfs_tree\fR
.TP
.B "jobid=<jobid>[,...]"
Job ID list (text; several values separated by commas)
.TP
.B "client=<client>"
Client name (latest state) (name; values from .clients)
.TP
.B "fileset=<fileset>"
FileSet name (name; values from .filesets)
.TP
.B "root=<root>"
Directory (text; default /)
.TP
.B "depth=<depth>"
Levels below root (number; default 1)
.TP
.B "max_entries=<max_entries>"
Most nodes in the result (number; default 100000)
.TP
.B "json"
Print the JSON object as text (keyword)
.PP
\fBExamples\fR
.TP
.B "\&.ovfs_tree jobid=600,615,616 root=/home/ depth=2"
two levels below /home of a job chain
.TP
.B "\&.ovfs_tree client=bareos\-fd depth=1 json"
the top level of the newest state as JSON
.PP
\fBOrigin\fR
Onesimus extension. The Bareos Director does not know this command and answers "is an invalid command".
.SS "\&.ovfs_ls"
Direct children of one directory of a job chain (OVFS)
.PP
\fBSynopsis\fR
.nf
\&.ovfs_ls [jobid=<jobid>[,...]] [client=<client>] [fileset=<fileset>] path=<path> [json]
.fi
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Onesimus extension: lists the direct entries of one directory in the state of a job chain, first the files, then the subdirectories with the number of their entries, so a restore browser can load the tree directory by directory. Directories below which nothing can be restored are left out. The chain is given by jobid= or by client= (and fileset=) for the newest state; path= is required and ends with a slash.
.PP
Each entry carries its restore identity (jobid, fileindex). json prints the result as one JSON line in every API mode.
.PP
\fBArguments\fR
.PP
\fI\&.ovfs_ls\fR
.TP
.B "jobid=<jobid>[,...]"
Job ID list (text; several values separated by commas)
.TP
.B "client=<client>"
Client name (latest state) (name; values from .clients)
.TP
.B "fileset=<fileset>"
FileSet name (name; values from .filesets)
.TP
.B "path=<path>"
Directory (text; required)
.TP
.B "json"
Print the JSON object as text (keyword)
.PP
\fBExamples\fR
.TP
.B "\&.ovfs_ls jobid=600,615,616 path=/etc/"
the entries of /etc in a job chain
.TP
.B "\&.ovfs_ls client=bareos\-fd path=/home/ json"
a directory of the newest state as JSON
.PP
\fBOrigin\fR
Onesimus extension. The Bareos Director does not know this command and answers "is an invalid command".
.SS "\&.ovfs_versions"
All versions of one file of a client (OVFS)
.PP
\fBSynopsis\fR
.nf
\&.ovfs_versions client=<client> path=<path> [name=<name>] [copies] [json]
.fi
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Onesimus extension: lists all backed up versions of a file or directory of a client, newest first. Only versions stored on a volume from backup and archive jobs count; copies adds copy jobs. Versions from jobs the console may not see are left out. path= is the directory, name= the file; without name= the versions of the directory itself are listed.
.PP
Each line has ten tab separated columns: JobId, level, job type, start time, size, modification time, FileIndex, volumes, InChanger and MD5. json prints the result as one JSON line. Unlike .bvfs_versions no PathIds are needed.
.PP
\fBArguments\fR
.PP
\fI\&.ovfs_versions\fR
.TP
.B "client=<client>"
Client name (name; values from .clients; required)
.TP
.B "path=<path>"
Directory (text; required)
.TP
.B "name=<name>"
File name ('' = the directory) (text)
.TP
.B "copies"
Include copy jobs (keyword)
.TP
.B "json"
Print the JSON object as text (keyword)
.PP
\fBExamples\fR
.TP
.B "\&.ovfs_versions client=bareos\-fd path=/etc/ name=hosts"
all versions of /etc/hosts
.TP
.B "\&.ovfs_versions client=bareos\-fd path=/etc/ name=fstab copies json"
including copy jobs, as JSON
.PP
\fBOrigin\fR
Onesimus extension. The Bareos Director does not know this command and answers "is an invalid command".
